WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.345
Last time, Alder's ratio came
out at 1.62, and Dune's at 1.46.

00:00:05.385 --> 00:00:08.862
So Alder looks the worse of the two.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.656
But put their curves side by side. Dune's
is the higher one, at every level of risk.

00:00:14.696 --> 00:00:18.737
Whoever you are, your chance of
readmission is greater at Dune.

00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:21.707
So how did Alder end up with the bigger ratio?

00:00:21.747 --> 00:00:26.854
The answer is in who each clinic treats.
And it leads to a second way of measuring.

00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:30.654
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:30.694 --> 00:00:32.730
Today: swap the patients.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.951
Start with lower-risk
patients. At the reference,

00:00:38.991 --> 00:00:44.147
their chance is 18%. At Alder
it is 34%, and at Dune, 36.

00:00:44.187 --> 00:00:46.947
At either clinic, that is close to double.

00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:53.597
Now the higher-risk patients: 62% at
the reference, 80 at Alder, 81 at Dune.

00:00:53.637 --> 00:00:57.892
A much smaller multiple, about
1.3. It is Episode 5 again:

00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:04.056
the same gap in odds means less, in
proportion, when the risk is already high.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.692
Here is the catch. Of Alder's
30 patients, 24 are lower-risk,

00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:13.248
where the multiple is near 2.
Dune's 12 are split evenly.

00:01:13.288 --> 00:01:17.024
So Alder's ratio is the larger
because of who Alder treats,

00:01:17.064 --> 00:01:19.462
not because its curve is higher.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.317
Give Dune Alder's patients,
and Dune's ratio would be 1.68.

00:01:24.357 --> 00:01:27.907
Last time's ratio judges each
clinic on its own patients.

00:01:27.947 --> 00:01:31.321
It does not line clinics
up against one another.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.321
So turn last time's method around. Keep
each clinic's curve, and swap the patients.

00:01:38.361 --> 00:01:41.190
Give every clinic the same crowd:

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:46.797
all 103 patients in the cast.
57 lower-risk, 46 higher-risk.

00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:51.871
Under Alder's curve, that
is 57 patients at 34%,

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:57.688
and 46 at 80%. Add up the
chances: 56.2 readmissions.

00:01:57.728 --> 00:02:02.713
56.2 out of 103. If
everyone had gone to Alder,

00:02:02.753 --> 00:02:07.486
the model expects 54.5% to be readmitted.

00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:12.207
That is Alder's direct rate. One
clinic's curve, everyone's patients.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:24.789
Now every clinic. Alder, 54.5%.
Birch, 32.2. Cedar, 37.6. Dune, 55.9.

00:02:24.829 --> 00:02:27.896
And Dune is back above
Alder. On the same patients,

00:02:27.936 --> 00:02:31.089
the higher curve always gives the higher rate.

00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:33.581
Elm's figure, 0.7%, is hatched.

00:02:33.621 --> 00:02:38.900
It rests on a gamma that only
records where the fitting stopped.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:44.979
One trap. The overall rate was 41%,
but that is not the yardstick here.

00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:49.852
Cedar, the reference clinic
itself, comes out at 37.6.

00:02:49.892 --> 00:02:55.964
Everyone at the reference would have 38.8
readmissions, the same 38.8 we met last time.

00:02:56.004 --> 00:03:01.975
So compare a direct rate with the reference
clinic's, not with the overall rate.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:13.281
Against Cedar's 37.6, Alder's 54.5 is 1.45
times as high. Last time's ratio said 1.62.

00:03:13.321 --> 00:03:16.359
Both are right. They
answer different questions.

00:03:17.933 --> 00:03:22.849
Last time's question: how did this clinic
do with the patients it actually treated?

00:03:22.889 --> 00:03:26.582
That is the one to ask when
a clinic is held to account.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.644
Today's question: how would this
clinic do with everyone's patients?

00:03:30.684 --> 00:03:35.065
That is closer to what someone
choosing between clinics wants to know.

00:03:35.105 --> 00:03:38.805
And neither is a rate that
anyone actually experienced.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.571
A common footing has a price. Alder
treated only 6 higher-risk patients.

00:03:45.611 --> 00:03:48.787
Its direct rate asks its curve about 46.

00:03:48.827 --> 00:03:54.595
Two-thirds of that 56.2 comes from
patients of a kind Alder hardly saw.

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:58.969
For Elm it is starker. Elm treated
no higher-risk patients at all.

00:03:59.009 --> 00:04:02.597
Its figure rests entirely on
the model's assumption that

00:04:02.637 --> 00:04:05.579
every clinic's curve has the same shape.

00:04:07.166 --> 00:04:10.656
In the crowd of 200, the
two measures agree closely.

00:04:10.696 --> 00:04:15.659
Yet between the two rankings, four
providers move ten places or more.

00:04:15.699 --> 00:04:19.975
A ranking can shift with the question,
before luck has even entered.

00:04:21.566 --> 00:04:23.062
Let's gather it up.

00:04:23.102 --> 00:04:26.902
Last time: a clinic's own
patients, on the reference curve.

00:04:26.942 --> 00:04:30.742
This time: everyone's patients,
on the clinic's own curve.

00:04:30.782 --> 00:04:32.847
The second compares like with like,

00:04:32.887 --> 00:04:36.034
but asks each clinic about
patients it never treated.

00:04:36.074 --> 00:04:38.769
Always say which one you are reporting.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:44.768
One question is still open. Alder had 13
readmissions where the reference expects 8.

00:04:44.808 --> 00:04:46.988
A real difference, or luck?

00:04:47.028 --> 00:04:49.383
Next time: unequal coins.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.605
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
